diff --git a/docs/KEYBOARD-SHORTCUTS.md b/docs/KEYBOARD-SHORTCUTS.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..e415c51
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/KEYBOARD-SHORTCUTS.md
@@ -0,0 +1,144 @@
+# PicoMarQ keyboard shortcuts
+
+Version 1.0.1. Windows keys are shown; on a Mac, read Cmd for Ctrl and Option for Alt.
+
+PicoMarQ has two views: the **formatted view** (the normal writing view, with the icon toolbar) and the **Markdown source view** (the raw text of your file). Shortcuts in the first two sections work in both views unless noted.
+
+## Files and the window
+
+| Keys | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| Ctrl+N | New document |
+| Ctrl+O | Open a file (several can be selected at once) |
+| Ctrl+S | Save (asks for a filename the first time) |
+| F12 | Save As |
+| Ctrl+W | Close the current document (asks about unsaved changes) |
+| Ctrl+P | Print |
+| Alt+F4 | Exit (asks about every unsaved document) |
+| Ctrl+Shift+M | Switch between the formatted view and the Markdown source view |
+| Ctrl+, | Settings |
+| Esc | Leave Settings |
+| Alt+F, Alt+E, Alt+V | Open the File, Edit, or View menu |
+
+Open Recent, Revert to Saved, and Apply Smart Typography have no shortcut; they are in the File and Edit menus.
+
+## Editing, finding, and zoom
+
+| Keys | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| Ctrl+Z | Undo |
+| Ctrl+Y | Redo (Ctrl+Shift+Z also redoes in the formatted view) |
+| Ctrl+X, Ctrl+C, Ctrl+V | Cut, copy, paste |
+| Ctrl+A | Select all |
+| Ctrl+F | Find (opens the Find bar, or moves the cursor back into it if it is already open) |
+| Ctrl+H | Find and Replace |
+| Ctrl+= or Ctrl+Shift+= or Ctrl+Numpad + | Zoom in (text only; menus and icons keep their size) |
+| Ctrl+- or Ctrl+Shift+- or Ctrl+Numpad - | Zoom out |
+| Ctrl+0 | Back to actual size |
+| Ctrl+mouse wheel | Zoom in and out |
+
+## Formatting (formatted view only)
+
+| Keys | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| Ctrl+B | Bold |
+| Ctrl+I | Italic |
+| Ctrl+U | Underline (saved as `<u>…</u>`, since Markdown has no underline of its own) |
+| Ctrl+Shift+S | Strikethrough |
+| Ctrl+E | Inline code (replaces any other formatting on the selection) |
+| Ctrl+K | Add a link to the selected text, or edit the link under the cursor |
+| Ctrl+Alt+1 through Ctrl+Alt+6 | Heading 1 through Heading 6 (press again to go back to normal text) |
+| Ctrl+Alt+0 | Normal paragraph |
+| Ctrl+Shift+B | Blockquote |
+| Ctrl+Shift+8 | Bullet list |
+| Ctrl+Shift+7 | Numbered list |
+| Ctrl+Shift+9 | Task list (checkboxes) |
+| Ctrl+Alt+C | Code block |
+| Shift+Enter or Ctrl+Enter | Line break inside a paragraph (without starting a new paragraph) |
+| Backspace, right after an automatic change | Undoes the change and gives you back what you typed (a heading made from "# ", a curly quote, an em dash, and so on) |
+
+Ctrl+K works only while the cursor is in the document, not in the Find bar or Settings.
+
+### Inside lists, tables, and code blocks
+
+| Where | Keys | What it does |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| List item | Enter | New item; Enter on an empty item ends the list |
+| List item | Tab | Indent the item one level |
+| List item | Shift+Tab | Outdent the item one level |
+| List item | Backspace at the start of the item | Lift the item out of the list |
+| Table | Tab | Next cell (in the last cell, adds a new row) |
+| Table | Shift+Tab | Previous cell |
+| Table | Backspace or Delete with every cell selected | Delete the table |
+| Code block | Enter three times at the end | Leave the code block |
+| Code block | Down arrow on the last line | Leave the code block |
+| Code block | Backspace in an empty block | Remove the block |
+| Math block (selected) | Enter or Space | Open the formula editor |
+
+Outside lists and tables, Tab moves the keyboard focus out of the document to the toolbar; it does not insert a tab character.
+
+## Typing shortcuts (formatted view)
+
+These are not key combinations but things the editor does as you type. Every one of them can be undone with Backspace immediately afterward.
+
+**At the start of a line**, followed by a space:
+
+| You type | You get |
+| --- | --- |
+| # through ###### | Heading 1 through Heading 6 |
+| -, *, or + | Bullet list |
+| 1. | Numbered list |
+| > | Blockquote |
+| [ ] or [x] | Task list item (unchecked or checked) |
+
+Also at the start of a line: three hyphens (---) make a horizontal rule, three backticks or three tildes followed by Enter start a code block, and a formula written between double dollar signs (\$\$ … \$\$) on a line of its own becomes a math block.
+
+**Anywhere in a line:**
+
+| You type | You get |
+| --- | --- |
+| \*\*bold\*\* or \_\_bold\_\_ | **bold** |
+| \*italic\* or \_italic\_ | *italic* |
+| \~\~struck\~\~ | ~~struck~~ |
+| text between single backticks | inline code |
+| \[text\](address) | a link |
+| a web address followed by a space | a link |
+
+**Smart typography** (Settings > Typography, on by default; works in both views):
+
+| You type | You get |
+| --- | --- |
+| Two hyphens (--) | An em dash (—). A third hyphen gives the three hyphens back, so a horizontal rule can still be typed. |
+| Three periods (...) | An ellipsis (…) |
+| " and ' | Curly quotes and apostrophes, opening or closing as the position requires |
+
+Edit > Apply Smart Typography converts a whole document at once, in a single undo step.
+
+## Find bar, link editor, formula editor
+
+| Where | Keys | What it does |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Find field | Enter | Next match |
+| Find field | Shift+Enter | Previous match |
+| Replace field | Enter | Replace this match |
+| Replace field | Ctrl+Enter | Replace all |
+| Find bar | Esc | Close the Find bar |
+| Link editor | Enter | Apply the link |
+| Link editor | Esc | Cancel |
+| Formula editor | Ctrl+Enter | Apply the formula |
+| Formula editor | Esc | Cancel |
+| Menu button (File, Edit, View) focused | Down arrow | Open that menu |
+| Menu button focused | Left or Right arrow | Move to the next menu |
+
+## Mouse
+
+| Action | What it does |
+| --- | --- |
+| Ctrl+click on a link | Open it in your browser (a plain click just places the cursor) |
+| Ctrl+mouse wheel | Zoom |
+| Right-click | Spelling suggestions for the word under the pointer, Add to Dictionary, Bold, Italic, Cut, Copy, Paste, and table commands when the cursor is in a table (in source view, Bold and Italic wrap the selection in Markdown markers) |
+| Double-click a page-edge handle | Return Page Width to Full (Settings > User Interface > Page Width, Custom) |
+
+## Markdown source view
+
+The file and editing shortcuts above all work. The formatting shortcuts do not (Ctrl+B does nothing here); write the Markdown instead. Smart typography still converts as you type, and Edit > Apply Smart Typography converts the whole file. The Find magnifier is not shown in this view; use Ctrl+F. Undo and redo in this view are the text box's own and are separate from the formatted view's history.
diff --git a/docs/test-documents/PicoMarQ-Torture-Test.md b/docs/test-documents/PicoMarQ-Torture-Test.md
new file mode 100644
index 0000000..ed9fd28
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/test-documents/PicoMarQ-Torture-Test.md
@@ -0,0 +1,143 @@
+---
+title: "PicoMarQ torture test"
+author: 'Ruth Example'
+note: Front matter stays exactly as typed -- quotes, hyphens, and "..." included.
+---
+
+# PicoMarQ Torture Test -- "Dumb" Typography Edition
+
+This file uses *straight* quotes, double hyphens, and three-period ellipses everywhere, on purpose. Open it, look at how everything renders, then choose **Edit > Apply Smart Typography** and watch the "dumb" marks turn into real ones. Ctrl+Z should put every one of them back in a single step.
+
+Ruth's rule of thumb: if it looks fine here, it'll look fine in a 200-page draft... probably.
+
+## Heading 2 -- Blocks of Text
+
+The sociological imagination, C. Wright Mills wrote, lets us grasp "history and biography and the relations between the two within society." That's the whole job in one sentence -- the rest is footnotes. A paragraph like this one tests ordinary body text: it wraps, it has 'single' and "double" quotes, it has a dash -- like that -- and it trails off...
+
+Here's a second paragraph with some **bold text**, some *italic text*, and some ***bold italic text***. Markdown also allows __bold with underscores__, _italics with underscores_, and ___both___. Mixing them inside a sentence -- **bold, then *italic inside bold*, then back** -- should hold together. There's ~~strikethrough~~ too, and `inline code "stays straight"` no matter what the button does.
+
+A sentence with a hard line break at the end of this line  
+should continue on the next line without starting a new paragraph.
+
+### Heading 3 -- Quotes in Awkward Places
+
+Apostrophes and quotes that trip up "smart" conversion:
+
+- The '90s, rock 'n' roll, and 'tis the season (the opening-quote cases)
+- Ruth's book, the students' essays, Durkheim's "Suicide" (the apostrophe cases)
+- A quote inside a quote: "She said 'no,' and that was that."
+- A quote after a dash -- "like this one" -- and after a parenthesis ("like this")
+- Height and distance written with primes: 5'10" and 12" of rain
+- Nested punctuation: ("quoted (and parenthesized)")... done.
+
+#### Heading 4 -- Italic, Regular Weight, Between Heading 3 and Body
+
+This heading should look smaller than Heading 3 above it, larger than this paragraph, italic, and not bold. If it looks like Heading 3, the style didn't take.
+
+## Blockquotes
+
+> "Society is not a mere sum of individuals. Rather, the system formed by their association represents a specific reality which has its own characteristics."
+>
+> -- Emile Durkheim, *The Rules of Sociological Method* (1895)
+
+> Goffman's point was simpler: "All the world is not, of course, a stage, but the crucial ways in which it isn't are not easy to specify."
+>
+> > A nested quote, with "quotes" of its own -- and a trailing ellipsis...
+
+## Lists
+
+### A numbered list
+
+1. Read the "methods" section first -- always.
+2. Check the sample. Who's missing?
+3. Look for the table that doesn't match the text...
+4. Then, and only then, read the conclusion.
+   1. A nested numbered item with 'quotes'
+   2. Another one -- with a dash
+
+### A numbered list that starts at five
+
+5. Fifth
+6. Sixth
+7. Seventh ("the one everyone skips")
+
+### A bulleted list
+
+- Structure: "the rules of the game"
+- Agency: what people do with them -- or against them
+- Culture
+  - Norms (the 'shoulds')
+  - Values (the 'whys')
+    - A third level, just to be difficult...
+- Institutions
+
+### A task list
+
+- [x] Headings 1 through 4
+- [x] Bold, italic, bold italic
+- [ ] Run Edit > Apply Smart Typography
+- [ ] Press Ctrl+Z and confirm everything comes back
+
+## A Table
+
+The separator row below uses `---` and `:--`, which must survive the button untouched. The cells contain "dumb" marks that should convert.
+
+| Thinker | Key work | Catchphrase | Year |
+|:--------|:--------:|-------------|-----:|
+| Durkheim | *Suicide* | "Social facts" -- treat them as things | 1897 |
+| Weber | *The Protestant Ethic* | The 'iron cage'... | 1905 |
+| Goffman | *Presentation of Self* | **Front stage** vs. back stage | 1956 |
+| Mills | *The Sociological Imagination* | ***Private troubles, public issues*** | 1959 |
+
+## Links and Images
+
+A plain hyperlink: [Project Gutenberg](https://www.gutenberg.org/). A link whose address contains two hyphens and whose title is in quotes -- both must stay untouched: [a link with a title](https://example.com/some--path "The link's title"). A bare address: <https://www.jacksonville.gov/>.
+
+An image embedded inside the file (a data URL), so it works offline:
+
+![Embedded image](data:image/svg+xml;base64,)
+
+An image from the folder next to this file (keep `picomarq-test-image.svg` beside the .md):
+
+![Relative image](picomarq-test-image.svg)
+
+An image from the internet (the upstream Scratch editor's screenshot; needs a connection):
+
+![Scratch screenshot](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/erictli/scratch/main/docs/screenshot.png)
+
+## Things the Button Must Leave Alone
+
+Inline code with "quotes" and -- dashes: `git commit -m "fix: don't -- ever..."`. Dollar signs are ordinary text, so this still converts: $5 an hour and 'overtime' at $10 -- "allegedly". And a block of code:
+
+```
+This fenced block is quoted "verbatim" -- nothing in here changes...
+After you run Edit > Apply Smart Typography, the body text above should contain:
+  “ ” curly double quotes      ‘ ’ curly single quotes
+  — an em dash                 … an ellipsis
+and this block, the inline code, the math, the table separators,
+the link addresses, and the front matter should be byte-for-byte the same.
+```
+
+Block math, also untouched:
+
+$$
+y' = \frac{dy}{dx} \quad \text{("the derivative")}
+$$
+
+A horizontal rule follows -- three hyphens on their own line, which must not become an em dash:
+
+---
+
+## Odds and Ends
+
+Special characters that are not quotes: the degree sign 20°C, the ampersand &, the percent 50%, copyright (c), a URL in the middle of a sentence https://www.gutenberg.org/ and an email like ruth.example@example.edu.
+
+Trailing matter: one final "quoted" word -- and an ellipsis to finish...
+
+## Known Limits of the Formatted View
+
+These render, but they do **not** survive a save from the formatted view (the source view keeps them). Smart typography leaves them alone either way.
+
+An HTML comment, invisible here but visible in the source view: <!-- don't "touch" this -- please... -->
+
+Escaped characters that should show the symbols themselves: \*not italic\*, \_not italic\_, \# not a heading, and a literal backslash \\.
